        "thread_goal": "Align on current SME tax review workflow and main pain points.",
        "messages": [
          {
            "msg_id": "M1",
            "speaker_id": 505737,
            "reply_to": null,
            "text": "Hi team, let's kick off the Standardised SME Tax Compliance Review Program. To start with T1, could everyone please briefly outline their current step-by-step process for reviewing an SME tax file? I've set up a draft document A1 to capture this. Let's also list any recurring pain points or bottlenecks you face.",
            "mentions": [
              235247,
              381305,
              544735,
              554843
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "T1",
              "A1"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M2",
            "speaker_id": 235247,
            "reply_to": "M1",
            "text": "Sure. I usually start by checking the trial balance against the source docs, then run through the main expense categories, calculate the tax, and fill out the forms. A big pain point for me is when the client's bookkeeping is messy, it takes ages to find the right numbers.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "white_check_mark",
                "count": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M3",
            "speaker_id": 554843,
            "reply_to": "M1",
            "text": "My process is similar: 1. Reconcile bank statements. 2. Verify major P&L accounts (revenue, COGS, payroll). 3. Review asset depreciation schedules. 4. Calculate tax liability. 5. Prepare the return. A major issue is inconsistent data formats from clients, especially with expense reports.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "thumbsup",
                "count": 2
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M4",
            "speaker_id": 381305,
            "reply_to": "M1",
            "text": "I do the same. I check the numbers and then do the tax form. Sometimes I'm not sure what to look for if it's a new type of business.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M5",
            "speaker_id": 544735,
            "reply_to": "M1",
            "text": "Adding to the above, I always perform a quick sanity check on the gross margin and compare it to previous years. It often flags issues early. A key pain point is the lack of a clear protocol for when a discrepancy is big enough to require a full re-check versus just a note.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "lightbulb",
                "count": 3
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M6",
            "speaker_id": 505737,
            "reply_to": "M5",
            "text": "That's a great point, a threshold for materiality is definitely something we need. Thanks everyone for the input. I've synthesized your steps into a single flow in A1. It looks like this:\n1. Source Doc Reconciliation (Bank, Invoices)\n2. P&L Account Verification\n3. Balance Sheet Review (Depreciation, Loans)\n4. Year-over-Year Sanity Check\n5. Tax Liability Calculation\n6. Return Form Preparation\nDoes this look right to everyone as our baseline current state?",
            "mentions": [
